    "topic": "how to pronounce GIF",
    "summary": "The GIF was developed in 1987 by Steve Wilhite at CompuServe. Wilhite insisted on a soft g ('jif'); the spec joked 'choosy programmers choose ... jif', after Jif peanut butter's slogan. At the 2013 Webby Awards his slide read 'It's pronounced jif not gif', and he told the New York Times the OED accepting both was wrong. Yet a 2013 Mashable poll of 30,706 people found seven in ten prefer the hard g, a 2017 Stack Overflow survey found 65.6% hard g, and in 2014 Obama said hard g. Dictionaries accept both.",
    "angle": "The inventor says jif. Most people say gif.",
